Chapter 6
Futures, forwards and options
العقود الآجلة والمستقبلية والخيارات
Orontes buys sesame. The price of sesame moves, and a move of a tenth takes more off its gross margin than three years of the decline Book 1 reported. The company cannot stop the price moving. What it can do is fix it in advance, or buy the right to a maximum, and those two sentences are the whole subject of this chapter.
A derivative is a contract whose value comes from something else — a commodity, a currency, an interest rate, a share, an index. It is not an investment in the ordinary sense: nobody owns anything when the contract is struck, and the contract exists only to move a risk from one party to another. This chapter sets out the four instruments the exam names, the two positions anybody can take in them, and the variables that decide what an option is worth.
Figure F606-01 is your map.
Figure F606-01. Chapter 6 roadmap: four contracts, two positions, six variables.
