WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 named foreign exchange risk, interest rate risk and market risk as three of the four risks a treasurer carries, and said that a manager hedges what a diversified shareholder would not pay to remove. These are the instruments that do the hedging.
6.1  What a derivative is, and what it is for
A derivative's value is derived from an underlying — the asset, rate or index the contract refers to. The contract itself creates no new asset. If one party gains a dollar the other loses a dollar, which is why a derivatives market is called a zero-sum game, and why its total size says nothing about the wealth in it.
Three uses, and the exam separates them sharply.
Hedging takes an existing exposure and offsets it. Orontes is already exposed to the sesame price by the simple fact of being a food manufacturer; a contract to buy sesame forward at a fixed price cancels part of that exposure. A hedger's gain on the contract is matched by a loss on the underlying business, and the point is not to make money on the contract but to make the outcome certain.
Speculation takes an exposure that did not exist. A trader with no sesame business who buys the same contract has created a position in the sesame price. The instrument is identical; the purpose, and the risk, are the opposite.
Arbitrage takes two prices for the same thing and locks in the difference. In theory it is riskless and in practice it is nearly so, and it is the force that keeps a derivative's price tied to its underlying.
A fourth motive deserves naming because questions use it: a derivative can change the form of a position cheaply. Converting floating-rate debt to fixed by swapping the interest, rather than repaying one bond and issuing another, saves the whole cost of an issue — and Chapter 7 takes that up.
Figure F606-02 contrasts the three purposes.
Figure F606-02. Three reasons to hold a derivative
@table
Purpose | The underlying exposure | What the contract does | Risk after the trade
Hedging | Already exists | Offsets it | Lower
Speculation | Does not exist | Creates it | Higher
Arbitrage | Two prices for one thing | Locks the difference | Close to nil
@end
EXAM TRAP   The same contract does opposite work
Whether a position is a hedge or a speculation depends on what else the holder owns, never on the contract. A question that describes a company buying a commodity forward for a commodity it does not use is describing a speculation.
A hedge removes the upside with the downside. A company that hedges and then watches prices fall has paid for certainty, not made a mistake.
Derivatives are off balance sheet in the sense that no asset is bought, and they are recognised at fair value under the standards. Part 1 covered the accounting; Section B covers the economics.
