Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
6.2  Forwards, futures, and the two positions
A forward contract is an agreement between two parties to buy or sell a stated quantity of something at a stated price on a stated future date. It is negotiated privately, so every term can be tailored, and it is settled once, at delivery.
A futures contract is the same promise, standardised and traded on an exchange. Quantity, quality, delivery date and delivery point are fixed by the exchange; the only thing negotiated is the price. The exchange's clearing house stands between the two parties, so neither takes the other's credit risk, and both post margin: an initial deposit, and then a daily settlement of gains and losses called marking to market.
Those two differences — customisation against standardisation, one settlement against daily settlement — generate every other difference the exam asks for.
Figure F606-03 compares them.
Figure F606-03. Forwards against futures
@table
Feature | Forward | Futures
Where traded | Privately, over the counter | On an exchange
Terms | Negotiated and tailored | Standardised
Counterparty risk | On the other party | On the clearing house
Margin | None usually | Initial and maintenance, settled daily
Settlement | Once, at maturity | Daily, by marking to market
Closing early | By agreement, or a new offsetting contract | By an offsetting trade, easily
Typical user | A company hedging a specific exposure | A company or a trader wanting liquidity
@end
The two positions are simple and are tested constantly. The long position is the buyer's: long means committed to buy, and a long gains when the price rises. The short position is the seller's: short means committed to sell, and a short gains when the price falls. A hedger chooses the position that offsets the business. Orontes buys sesame, so a price rise hurts it, so it goes long. A farmer sells sesame, so a price rise helps, so the farmer goes short — and the farmer and Orontes are the two sides of the same contract, each made safer by it.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: Orontes fixes its sesame price
Step 1. The exposure. Orontes expects to buy 1,000 tonnes of sesame in six months. The current price is $1,200 a tonne, so the expected cost is 1,000 × 1,200 = 1,200,000. A rise of a tenth would add $120,000 to cost of sales, which is more than a point of gross margin.
Step 2. The hedge. The company buys 1,000 tonnes six months forward at $1,200. It is now long sesame futures and long the physical requirement, and the two offset.
Step 3. The price rises. Suppose sesame reaches $1,350. The physical purchase costs 1,350 − 1,200 = 150 a tonne more than planned, which is 150 × 1,000 = 150,000. The contract gains the identical $150,000. Net cost: $1,200 a tonne, as planned.
Step 4. The price falls. Suppose sesame falls to $1,100. The physical purchase is $100 a tonne cheaper, saving $100,000, and the contract loses $100,000. Net cost: $1,200 a tonne, as planned.
Check. In both directions Orontes paid $1,200 a tonne, which is what hedging means. The company gave up a $100,000 windfall to avoid a $150,000 shock, and whether that is a good trade depends on whether certainty is worth more to it than the average outcome — which for a company with a gross margin of 35 per cent and a bank covenant to respect, it generally is. Had Orontes bought futures rather than a forward, it would also have had to fund margin: at an initial margin of 5 per cent, 1,200,000 × 0.05 = 60,000 of cash tied up, plus whatever the daily settlements demanded.
