6.3  Options: calls, puts, and what they cost
A forward obliges. An option entitles. That single word is the difference, and it is why an option costs money up front and a forward does not.
A call option gives its holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying at a stated price on or before a stated date. A put option gives the right to sell. The stated price is the strike price or exercise price. The date is the expiration. The money paid for the right is the premium, and it is paid whether or not the option is ever used.
Every option has two sides. The holder, who paid the premium, has a right and no obligation; the writer, who received it, has an obligation and no right. The holder's loss is limited to the premium and the gain is open-ended on a call; the writer's gain is limited to the premium and the loss is open-ended. The exam checks that direction more often than any other point in the chapter.
Two styles appear: an American option may be exercised at any time up to expiration, a European option only at expiration. The American style is worth at least as much, because it allows everything the European allows and more.
Intrinsic value is what the option would be worth if exercised immediately, and it is never negative. For a call it is the underlying price less the strike, or zero if that is negative. For a put it is the strike less the underlying price, or zero. An option with intrinsic value is in the money; with none and the underlying at the strike, at the money; with none and the underlying the wrong side, out of the money.
Time value is the rest of the premium: what a buyer pays for the chance that the option moves further into the money before expiration. Premium equals intrinsic value plus time value, always, and at expiration time value is zero so the premium equals the intrinsic value.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a call on sesame
Step 1. The contract. Instead of a forward, Orontes buys a six-month call on 1,000 tonnes of sesame with a strike of $1,250 a tonne, paying a premium of $40 a tonne.
Step 2. Prices rise sharply. At $1,350, the call is in the money: its intrinsic value is 1,350 − 1,250 = 100 a tonne, and after the premium the gain is 100 − 40 = 60 a tonne. Orontes exercises and its effective cost is $1,290 a tonne.
Step 3. Prices fall. At $1,100, the call is worthless and Orontes lets it expire, losing the $40 premium and buying in the market at $1,100. Its effective cost is $1,140 a tonne — $60 better than the forward of section 6.2.
Step 4. The breakeven. The call buyer is better off than doing nothing above 1,250 + 40 = 1,290, and worse off below it by the premium.
Check. Compare the three strategies at the two prices. Unhedged, Orontes pays $1,350 or $1,100. Forward, it pays $1,200 either way. Call, it pays $1,290 or $1,140. The option is the only one that caps the bad outcome and keeps the good one, and the premium of $40 a tonne — $40,000 in total — is exactly what that asymmetry costs. There is no free version.
Figure F606-04 sets the three side by side.
Figure F606-04. One exposure, three strategies
@table
Sesame at delivery | Unhedged cost a tonne | With the forward at 1,200 | With the 1,250 call at a 40 premium
1,100 | 1,100 | 1,200 | 1,140
1,200 | 1,200 | 1,200 | 1,240
1,350 | 1,350 | 1,200 | 1,290
Worst case | Unlimited | 1,200, fixed | 1,290, capped
@end
EXAM TRAP   Who has the right, and who has the obligation
The holder of a call or a put has the right. The writer has the obligation and keeps the premium whatever happens.
Intrinsic value is never negative. An out-of-the-money option has an intrinsic value of zero, not a negative one.
A call is not the opposite of a put. The opposite of buying a call is writing a call. Buying a put is a different position entirely.
Figure F606-05 sets out the four positions.
Figure F606-05. Who holds the right, and who carries the obligation
@table
Position | Right or obligation | Maximum gain | Maximum loss
Buyer of a call | The right to buy at the strike | Unlimited | The premium paid
Writer of a call | The obligation to sell at the strike | The premium received | Unlimited
Buyer of a put | The right to sell at the strike | The strike less the premium | The premium paid
Writer of a put | The obligation to buy at the strike | The premium received | The strike less the premium
@end
