6.4  What an option is worth
Six variables determine an option's premium, and the outcome asks for the direction of each. Taking a call first.
The price of the underlying. Higher is better for a call: more intrinsic value. Worse for a put.
The strike price. Lower is better for a call, because the right to buy cheaply is worth more. Higher is better for a put.
Time to expiration. More is better for both. Time is the chance of a favourable move, and since the downside is capped at the premium, more chances can only help.
Volatility of the underlying. More is better for both, and this is the one candidates find counter-intuitive. A more volatile underlying produces more extreme outcomes in both directions, and the option holder keeps the good extremes and is protected from the bad ones. Volatility is therefore the variable option traders actually trade.
The risk-free rate. A higher rate is slightly better for a call, because the strike will be paid later and money paid later is worth less; slightly worse for a put, by the same reasoning.
Dividends on the underlying. A dividend lowers the share price on the ex-dividend date, so expected dividends reduce a call's value and raise a put's.
Figure F606-06 lays the six out.
Figure F606-06. What moves an option premium
@table
Variable | An increase in it | Effect on a call | Effect on a put
Price of the underlying | Up | Up | Down
Strike price | Up | Down | Up
Time to expiration | Up | Up | Up
Volatility | Up | Up | Up
Risk-free rate | Up | Up slightly | Down slightly
Expected dividends | Up | Down | Up
@end
Two further relationships appear in questions. The premium can never be less than the intrinsic value, because anybody could otherwise buy the option and exercise it at once for a profit — the arbitrage of section 6.1 at work. And a call and a put with the same strike and expiration are linked to the underlying and a risk-free bond by put-call parity, which is the formal statement that the same payoff can be built two ways and must therefore cost the same.
Note finally what is not on the list. The expected return on the underlying is not there, and neither is anybody's opinion about where the price is going. That is the surprising result at the heart of option pricing, and it holds because the option can be replicated by holding the underlying and borrowing, so its price is fixed by arbitrage rather than by forecasts.
