6.5  Choosing an instrument
A treasurer with an exposure has four honest answers and the exam expects the choice to be argued rather than asserted.
Do nothing. Appropriate when the exposure is small against the business, when the company is naturally hedged because its costs and revenues move together, or when the cost of hedging exceeds the risk removed. Doing nothing is a decision and should be recorded as one.
Hedge with a forward or a future. Appropriate when the exposure is known in amount and timing and the company wants certainty. It costs nothing up front and it removes the upside. Choose a forward when the amount or date is specific, a future when liquidity and credit safety matter more than an exact fit.
Hedge with an option. Appropriate when the exposure is uncertain — a tender that may not be won, a harvest that may not arrive — or when the company wants protection and is willing to pay for keeping the upside. It costs a premium, and that premium is the whole of what distinguishes it from a forward.
Hedge naturally. Match the exposure inside the business: borrow in the currency you earn, buy from a supplier whose price moves with your selling price, hold inventory as a hedge against a rising input cost. This is the cheapest hedge available and the exam rewards the candidate who mentions it first.
Two warnings close the chapter.
Basis risk. A hedge is rarely exact. If the contract's underlying is not precisely what the company buys — a sesame future against a specific grade, or a crude oil future against jet fuel — the two prices move together but not identically, and the difference between them is basis risk, which no hedge of that kind removes.
A hedge that becomes a speculation. A company that hedges more than its exposure, or keeps a contract after the exposure has gone, is speculating with the shareholders' money. Every famous derivatives loss in corporate history began as an authorised hedging programme, and the control that prevents it is a policy limiting positions to documented exposures.
