LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Short"
English short in finance means committed to deliver something you do not hold: a short position gains when prices fall. It has nothing to do with time, although short-term debt is a phrase in the same book, and nothing to do with a shortage, although a short seller may create one. Arabic uses مركز البيع, literally a selling position, which states the meaning plainly and avoids the metaphor entirely. French position courte is a direct borrowing of the English and carries the same opacity, while vendeur à découvert spells it out. Note the companion pair: to cover a short is to close it by buying, and a short squeeze is what happens when many shorts are forced to cover at once — both phrases appear in exam questions on market mechanics without explanation.
