Chapter summary
Figure F606-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F606-08. Chapter 6 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A derivative takes its value from an underlying and creates no asset, so one side's gain is the other's loss; the same contract hedges an existing exposure, speculates on one that did not exist, or arbitrages two prices, and only what else the holder owns says which. Forwards are private and tailored and settle once; futures are standardised, cleared, margined and marked to market daily — and in both, long means committed to buy and gains when prices rise, short means committed to sell and gains when they fall. A forward obliges and an option entitles: a call is the right to buy at the strike, a put the right to sell, the premium is paid either way, intrinsic value is never negative, and premium equals intrinsic plus time value. Orontes's 1,000 tonnes show the whole trade-off: unhedged it pays 1,100 or 1,350 a tonne, forward it pays 1,200 either way, and with a 1,250 call at a premium of 40 it pays 1,140 or 1,290 — the only strategy that caps the bad outcome and keeps the good one, at a cost of exactly $40,000. Six variables move an option's premium, of which volatility is the one traders trade and the expected return on the underlying is conspicuously not among them. And the best hedge of all is usually the natural one inside the business, while the worst risk in a hedging programme is the hedge that outlives its exposure.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
