Case-style practice: hedging the sesame price
Orontes's board is asked to approve a hedging policy for its sesame purchases, which run at about 12,000 tonnes a year at around $1,200 a tonne. The treasurer proposes buying forward the whole of the next twelve months' requirement. The chief executive prefers call options at a strike 4 per cent above the current price, costing about $40 a tonne. A director asks why the company should hedge at all when its shareholders are diversified.
Required. Set out the cost and the effect of each proposal, answer the director, and recommend a policy. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC6-1  Answer A. The value is derived from the underlying.
B is wrong. Derivatives are contracts, not claims on a company's assets.
C is wrong. The exchange provides the market, not the value.
D is wrong. The contract's cash flows are themselves functions of the underlying.
SC6-2  Answer A. The exposure already exists in the business.
B is wrong. Speculation creates an exposure that did not exist.
C is wrong. Arbitrage exploits two prices for one thing.
D is wrong. Buying a forward is taking a position, not issuing one.
SC6-3  Answer A. Certainty is bought by giving up the favourable tail.
B is wrong. A hedge is judged before the outcome, not after.
C is wrong. The gain on the underlying is offset by the loss on the hedge.
D is wrong. Nothing cancels automatically.
SC6-4  Answer A. Long means committed to buy.
B is wrong. That is the short position.
C is wrong. A flat price produces no gain on either side.
D is wrong. Volatility alone does not determine the direction of a forward's payoff.
SC6-5  Answer A. Daily settlement through a clearing house is the futures feature.
B is wrong. Both fix a price in advance.
C is wrong. Both settle in the future.
D is wrong. Both oblige both parties.
SC6-6  Answer A. A future buyer hedges a price rise by going long.
B is wrong. A short gains when prices fall, which is the seller's hedge.
C is wrong. No position leaves the exposure open.
D is wrong. Writing a put obliges the company to buy at the strike and does not cap its cost.
SC6-7  Answer A. A call is the right to buy.
B is wrong. That is a put.
C is wrong. Options carry no dividend rights.
D is wrong. Options carry no votes.
SC6-8  Answer A. 1,350 − 1,250 = 100.
B is wrong. The option is in the money.
C is wrong. That is the underlying price.
D is wrong. 40 is the premium in the example.
SC6-9  Answer A. The buyer can lose the premium and no more.
B is wrong. Unlimited loss belongs to the writer of a call.
C is wrong. The strike is paid only on exercise, in exchange for the asset.
D is wrong. Intrinsic value is a component of value, not a loss.
SC6-10  Answer A. More extreme outcomes help a holder whose downside is capped.
B is wrong. Volatility helps both.
C is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
D is wrong. Volatility is the principal driver of time value.
SC6-11  Answer A. Otherwise an immediate exercise would be a riskless profit.
B is wrong. Time value is the part above intrinsic value.
C is wrong. The strike is not a floor on the premium.
D is wrong. The underlying price is not a floor on the premium.
SC6-12  Answer A. Costs and revenues move together inside the business.
B is wrong. Nothing new has been exposed.
C is wrong. Basis risk arises from an imperfect contract, not from matching.
D is wrong. No option has been bought or sold.
SC6-13  Answer A. An imperfect match between the contract and the exposure.
B is wrong. An oversized hedge is a speculation, not basis risk.
C is wrong. Margin is a funding matter.
D is wrong. Time value concerns options, not basis.
SC6-14  Answer A. An option can be abandoned if the exposure never arises.
B is wrong. A forward would oblige performance on an exposure that may not exist.
C is wrong. A short future is the wrong direction and still an obligation.
D is wrong. Doing nothing leaves the exposure open if the tender is won.
Your turn
@table
Position | Answer
Intrinsic value of a call | 8: 58 − 50 = 8.
Intrinsic value of a put | Nil: the strike is below the underlying, so exercising would sell at 50 something worth 58.
Time value of that call | 3: 11 − 8 = 3.
Breakeven for the call buyer | 61: 50 + 11 = 61.
Maximum loss of the call buyer | 11, the premium paid.
Maximum loss of the call writer | Unlimited, because the underlying can rise without limit.
Position for a seller of wheat | Short: sell wheat futures, so that a fall in the wheat price is offset by a gain on the contract.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P6-01  Answer A. The value derives from the underlying.
B is wrong. A derivative is not a claim on an issuer's assets.
C is wrong. Only the contract terms are fixed; the value moves.
D is wrong. The value changes with the underlying.
P6-02  Answer A. Futures are marked to market daily.
B is wrong. Both fix the price in advance.
C is wrong. Both settle in the future.
D is wrong. Both are obligations.
P6-03  Answer A. Long gains on a rise.
B is wrong. That is the short position.
C is wrong. A stable price produces no gain.
D is wrong. Volatility matters to options, not to the direction of a future's payoff.
P6-04  Answer A. A put is the right to sell.
B is wrong. That is a call.
C is wrong. The writer receives the premium.
D is wrong. Options carry no dividend rights.
P6-05  Answer A. 58 − 50 = 8.
B is wrong. The call is in the money.
C is wrong. 50 is the strike.
D is wrong. 58 is the underlying price.
P6-06  Answer A. 11 − 8 = 3.
B is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
C is wrong. That is the whole premium.
D is wrong. That is the intrinsic value.
P6-07  Answer A. More time is more chance, and the downside is capped.
B is wrong. Puts gain from time as well.
C is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
D is wrong. Time value is a large part of most premiums.
P6-08  Answer A. A written call has an open-ended loss.
B is wrong. That is the maximum gain.
C is wrong. The strike is received on exercise.
D is wrong. The writer's risk is the largest in the chapter.
P6-09  Answer A. A future receipt of foreign currency is hedged by selling it forward.
B is wrong. Buying would double the exposure.
C is wrong. A call protects a buyer of euros, not a receiver.
D is wrong. The amount in dollars is uncertain, which is the exposure.
P6-10  Answer A. With no exposure, the position is a bet.
B is wrong. A hedge requires something to hedge.
C is wrong. Arbitrage locks a price difference.
D is wrong. A natural hedge is an offset inside the business.
Case-style set: hedging the sesame price
The forward proposal. Twelve thousand tonnes at $1,200 fixes an annual cost of about $14,400,000. It costs nothing up front, removes the whole of the price risk in both directions, and requires the company to take delivery or settle whatever happens. Its real cost is the upside forgone: if sesame fell a tenth, Orontes would pay about $1,440,000 more than the market.
The option proposal. Calls struck 4 per cent above the current price, at $40 a tonne, cost 12,000 × 40 = 480,000 for the year. They cap the cost at the strike plus the premium, which is about $1,288 a tonne, and leave the whole of any fall to the company. The premium is about 1.1 per cent of the purchase cost and about 3.3 per cent of a year's gross profit.
The director's question. The director is right in theory and wrong in this company. A diversified shareholder does not need Orontes to hedge a sesame price, because the shareholder can hold the farm as well. But Orontes is not only its shareholders: it has a bank covenant measured on reported ratios, a gross margin that Book 1 found falling a point a year, and managers whose attention is a finite resource. A price shock that breached a covenant or forced an emergency repricing would cost the company more than the hedge does, and none of that cost is diversifiable by anybody.
The recommendation. Hedge the firm commitments with forwards — the volumes already contracted to customers at agreed prices, where the exposure is certain and certainty is what is wanted. Use options for the uncertain remainder, where the volume depends on orders not yet won. And write the policy to limit positions to documented exposures, so that the hedging programme can never become a trading one.
